# Lumen Supply demo app - example environment. Copy this file to `.env`
# (git-ignored). It is loaded by prisma.config.ts for the Prisma CLI (migrate,
# seed) and by Next.js for the storefront; a variable already set in the
# process environment wins over it. This workspace never opens the
# repository-root `.env` itself, but a dev server the local service launches
# inherits the service's environment, which includes what the service loaded
# from the root `.env` - so a root DATABASE_URL or NEXT_OTEL_VERBOSE reaches
# a service-launched storefront and wins over this file. Keep the two
# DATABASE_URL copies identical.
#
# DATABASE_URL and APP_ORIGIN are the two variables to set here. The workspace
# also reads PORT, which `next dev` / `next start` set to the port they bound
# (choose it with `next dev -p <port>`, never in this file), and Next.js itself
# reads NEXT_OTEL_VERBOSE, an optional tracing switch documented in the root
# .env.example. The repository README's "Environment variables" section lists
# every variable with its reader, type and default.
#
# The DATABASE_URL value is the local Docker Compose development credential: it
# matches the `postgres` service in docker-compose.yml, reaches nothing but that
# throwaway container and is not a secret, so keep it character for character.
# It has no default: without it `migrate` and `seed` stop and the storefront's
# queries fail to connect.
#
# Run this workspace's scripts as `pnpm --filter @debuggen/demo-app <script>`;
# `pnpm demo:migrate`, `demo:seed`, `demo:generate` and `demo:dev` are the root
# aliases for four of them.
#
# Scripts that need a real, reachable database, so DATABASE_URL must be set:
#   migrate      `prisma migrate deploy` - applies prisma/migrations
#   migrate:dev  `prisma migrate dev` - the authoring path, writes migrations
#   seed         `prisma db seed`
#   dev, start   the storefront's own queries
#
# Scripts that work with no database and no DATABASE_URL at all, which is what
# keeps a clean clone buildable:
#   generate, build, typecheck   `prisma generate` opens no connection
#   check:migration              renders prisma/schema.prisma to SQL offline
#                                and compares it with the committed migration
#
# check:migration verifies and writes nothing. Regenerating
# prisma/migrations/0001_init/migration.sql is still a redirection of
# `prisma migrate diff --from-empty --to-schema prisma/schema.prisma --script`
# into the file; run check:migration straight afterwards, because a redirection
# writes whatever the CLI emitted and only that comparison establishes that the
# file is the complete schema. It runs by itself as the `premigrate` step of
# `migrate`, so a migration that no longer matches is caught before it is
# applied. prisma.config.ts is what gives that offline diff a datasource, and
# its comments explain how.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://debuggen:debuggen@localhost:5432/lumen_supply

# The scheme, host and port the storefront is served on.
#
# Checkout reaches the simulated carrier and payment processor over HTTP, the
# way it would reach real ones, and a route handler can only learn where to
# address them from the request it is answering - which is a value the caller
# chose. Naming the store here is what stops a request from redirecting those
# two calls somewhere else: `src/server/origin.ts` reads this variable and
# refuses any other authority, so set it to the address the app actually
# answers on, port included, and change it when you change the port.
#
# The scheme and the port are matched exactly; the three names for this machine
# (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `[::1]`) count as one address when the variable
# names one of them, because a server reports whichever it prefers - Next's dev
# server says `localhost` however it was reached. So the value below is right
# for `next dev` on its default port, and a run on another port needs the port
# here changed to match.
#
# Left unset, the store accepts only an http authority whose host is exactly
# `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` or `[::1]`, on any port, and a page rendered on the
# server addresses its own reads to http://127.0.0.1:<PORT>. That keeps
# `pnpm demo:dev` working on whichever port it was started on without
# configuration. Any other host - a LAN address, a custom host name, https or a
# proxy in front of the dev server - is refused, so set the variable to the
# exact origin the store answers on in those cases. It is the development
# convenience; any machine the store shares with another listener should set
# the variable too.
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
